### Page 6 of 15

```text
UDC-23-00074
May 1, 2023
The location associated with three major streets (Converse, Pershing, and 19th) would encourage
the development of commercial uses in this area. PlanCheyenne encourages the flexibility to be
responsive to market demands. PlanCheyenne also encourages the conversion of homes along
arterials to more commercial-style uses.

The Future Land Use Designation for this property and the surrounding area north of Pershing is
Urban Residential. According to comprehensive plan the optimal City zone for the subject
is any one of the residential zoning districts. However,
the future land use map shouldn t be construed as having bright lines when it comes to future
land use designations, and the property is close to a mixed-use commercial designation at a
major intersection. The expansion of mixed-use commercial type uses to this property is in
concordance with the general guide of the future land use map.

The policies of PlanCheyenne are supportive of this proposed zone change. The future land use
plan encourages commercial-style zoning at this general location.

The proposed zone change largely complies with review criterion 1.

2) The application is compatible with nearby property, specifically considering existing zoning,
the Comprehensive Plan, and any other official plans and policies created under the guidance
of that plan for these areas; (UDC 2.2.1.d.2)

Surrounding property are zoned with the MUB and MR zones. MUB is the current zoning of the
parcels immediately to the south and east. The proposed zoning is also compatible with the
nearby future land use designation of Mixed-use Commercial and PlanCheyenne s
encouragement of converting homes along major roadways to commercial uses.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 2.

3) The suitability of the subject property for development according to the standards and uses of
the existing zoning district, compared to the suitability of the subject property for
development according to the standards and uses of the proposed zoning district (UDC
2.2.1.d.3)

The current MR zone encourages a variety of residential uses. The MUB zone allows all
residential uses the MR zone does, but also allows a variety of commercial uses. The MUB zone
encourages buildings to be built close to the street with limited side parking and primarily rear
parking areas. The proximity to Pershing and the roundabout  and the narrow nature of the
parcel  means that vehicular access to Pershing would not be allowed. The rear-loaded nature
of MUB zoning development is appropriate at this location.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 3.

4) The ability of the subject property and surrounding vicinity to be developed according to the
Subdivision Standards in Article 4 considering the proposed zoning (UDC 2.2.1.d.4)

Block sizes are typically based on the future land use designation, and that is not proposed to be
changed with this application. However, the intent statement of the MUB zone generally
```

### Page 7 of 15

```text
UDC-23-00074
May 1, 2023
requires smaller blocks than the MR zone, but there is limited opportunity to create any
meaningful connections to the street network due to the previous development patterns. The
MUB zone is generally more restrictive in direct access to streets, which is appropriate at this
location due to the proximity to the roundabout.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 4.

5) The ability of the application to support the Intent and Applicability statements of the
proposed zoning district and achieve the Design Objectives of any specific standards. (UDC
2.2.1.d.5)

The MUB zone is intended to develop with a mix of residential, retail, employment, service, and
civic uses in activity centers or areas designated Mixed-use Commercial by the comprehensive
plan. Land in the area is designated as Mixed-use Commercial by PlanCheyenne.

MUB design standards encourage buildings located near the street with parking behind. Should
the property redevelop, the access off of Pershing would be required to be closed with parking
on the rear part of the parcel  due to the proximity to the roundabout, the redevelopment of
the parcel would likely required the closing of the Pershing access  no matter the zone or the
use.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 5.

6) The ability of all potential future development to meet all applicable standards for
development in the proposed zoning district, including other development options if any
specific development proposed in association with the application is not executed after
approval. (UDC 2.2.1.d.6)

It is Staff s understanding the specific development proposed in concert with this rezoning is the
conversion of the existing house to a commercial use. Should the proposed development not be
executed after approval, there are a number of commercial and residential uses which would be
eligible to be established at this location. Conversion back into a single-family home would also
be possible.

The proposed zone change complies with review criterion 6.

7) The review and recommendations of other reviewing bodies. (UDC 2.2.1.d.7)

No reviewing agencies provided recommendations regarding the proposed zone change.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 7.

8) Whether the application will harm the public health, safety, or general welfare. (UDC
2.2.1.d.8)

City staff is satisfied that this proposed zone change would not adversely affect the public
health, safety, or general welfare.

The proposed zone change for the subject property complies with review criterion 8.
```

